I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the correction." The two replies are, in full, Reply A: the string consisting of three backticks, the word json, a newline, the text {"a": 1}, a newline, the text {"b": 2}, a newline, three backticks, a newline, and the text {"c": 3}. Reply B: the string {"c": 3}. The rule requires Reply A to recover {"b": 2}, because a code fence holds objects and the last object inside that fence is {"b": 2}. The rule requires Reply B to recover {"c": 3}, because there is no fence and the last top-level object is {"c": 3}. The check gives the same answer for both: it returns ({"c": 3}, ()). For Reply A, the fenced pool sees the whole fenced content as one candidate, which fails to parse as a single object, so the check falls through to the top-level pool and returns the later outside object {"c": 3}. I would be wrong if recover_json_object on Reply A actually returned {"b": 2} (or raised), or if on Reply B it returned something other than {"c": 3}.